Chapter: GpuRender
  cites UI chapter Theme
  cites UI chapter BoxModel
  cites UI chapter Widget
  cites UI chapter Overlay
  cites UI chapter TextOverflow

   depth 10000 = scene (background geometry)
   depth 5000  = chrome (toolbar, sidebar, status bar text)
   depth 2000  = overlay panels (dropdown menus, popups)
   depth 1000  = overlay text (menu items, tooltip text)

Section: GPU Constants

  gr-cmd : Integer = #BE000000

Section: GPU Primitives

 Composing a triangle is a Gpu.Memory effect, not a bare memory write.
 gpu-mem-write is poke-32 with the honest row: a caller must declare
 Gpu.Memory, which is what earns the cap-gpu-memory bit the kernel now
 tests on the window at 0xBE000000. Reaching the same window through
 raw poke-32 is still refused without the bit -- the type is what makes
 the requirement visible before you run into the refusal.

 An effectful value may not be let-bound (CDX2033), and every write
 here is effectful now, so the eighteen words of a triangle are one
 arithmetic expression rather than eighteen let-bindings. Addition is
 left-associative and arguments evaluate left to right, so the words
 still land in offset order -- and they are distinct offsets anyway.
 The same rule is why the callers below nest their calls instead of
 binding them: a call in argument or tail position is fine, only a
 let-binding is not.

  gr-tri : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Gpu.Memory] Integer
  gr-tri (idx) (x0) (y0) (x1) (y1) (x2) (y2) (c0) (c1) (c2) (depth) =
    let off = idx * 72
    in gpu-mem-write gr-cmd off x0 + gpu-mem-write gr-cmd (off + 4) y0 + gpu-mem-write gr-cmd (off + 8) x1 + gpu-mem-write gr-cmd (off + 12) y1 + gpu-mem-write gr-cmd (off + 16) x2 + gpu-mem-write gr-cmd (off + 20) y2 + gpu-mem-write gr-cmd (off + 24) c0 + gpu-mem-write gr-cmd (off + 28) c1 + gpu-mem-write gr-cmd (off + 32) c2 + gpu-mem-write gr-cmd (off + 36) depth + gpu-mem-write gr-cmd (off + 40) depth + gpu-mem-write gr-cmd (off + 44) depth + gpu-mem-write gr-cmd (off + 48) 0 + gpu-mem-write gr-cmd (off + 52) 0 + gpu-mem-write gr-cmd (off + 56) 0 + gpu-mem-write gr-cmd (off + 60) 0 + gpu-mem-write gr-cmd (off + 64) 0 + gpu-mem-write gr-cmd (off + 68) 0

  gr-rect : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Gpu.Memory] Integer
  gr-rect (idx) (x) (y) (w) (h) (color) (depth) =
    gr-tri idx x y (x + w) y x (y + h) color color color depth + gr-tri (idx + 1) (x + w) y (x + w) (y + h) x (y + h) color color color depth

Section: Depth Constants

  gr-depth-scene : Integer = 10000
  gr-depth-chrome : Integer = 5000
  gr-depth-overlay : Integer = 2000
  gr-depth-top : Integer = 1000

Section: Frame Control

 Commanding the rasterizer is a Gpu.Compute effect, not a bare port
 write. gpu-out and gpu-in are port-out-32 and port-in-32 with the
 honest row: a caller must declare Gpu.Compute, which is what earns
 the cap-gpu-compute bit the kernel now tests on ports 0x400-0x417.
 Reaching the same window through raw port-out-32 is still refused
 without the bit -- the type is what makes the requirement visible
 before you run into the refusal.

  gr-clear : Integer -> [Gpu.Compute] Integer
  gr-clear (color) = gpu-out #401 color

  gr-clear-depth : [Gpu.Compute] Integer
  gr-clear-depth = gpu-out #402 0

  gr-flush : Integer -> [Gpu.Compute] Integer
  gr-flush (count) = gpu-out #400 count

Section: Widget Rendering

 A child renders one step IN FRONT of its parent, and anything drawn over
 a fill it overlaps does too. The rasterizer's test is a strict
 depth < stored (codex-vm.c, gpu_raster_tri), so a rect at the SAME depth
 as one already covering that pixel is discarded rather than blended. Draw
 a whole tree at one depth and only the root survives: every descendant
 loses to the fill its parent just wrote, geometry is submitted, the
 triangle count looks healthy, and the frame is empty. That is how the
 GuiOS desktop came to render nothing but its CPU-drawn text, and it is
 the same defect that blanked the circuits SIM tab.

 Siblings share a depth deliberately. A layout gives them disjoint rects,
 so they never contend, and spending a level per sibling would exhaust the
 band. The step is one, which is exact: a rect carries the same depth at
 all three vertices, and the edge functions at any interior point sum to
 the doubled area, so the interpolated depth is that value with no
 rounding. Bands are 1000 apart (scene, chrome, overlay, top), which
 leaves room for a tree far deeper than any real one.

  GpuFrame = record {
    gf-idx : Integer,
    gf-max : Integer
  }

  gf-new : Integer -> GpuFrame
  gf-new (max-tris) = GpuFrame { gf-idx = 0, gf-max = max-tris }

  gr-render-tree : GpuFrame, WidgetNode, Theme, Integer -> [Gpu.Memory] GpuFrame
  gr-render-tree (gf) (w) (th) (depth) =
    gr-render-children (gr-render-node gf w th depth) (w.wn-children) th depth 0 (w.wn-child-count)

  gr-render-children : GpuFrame, List WidgetNode, Theme, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Gpu.Memory] GpuFrame
  gr-render-children (gf) (children) (th) (depth) (i) (n) =
    if i >= n then gf
    else gr-render-children (gr-render-tree gf (list-at children i) th (depth - 1)) children th depth (i + 1) n

  gr-render-node : GpuFrame, WidgetNode, Theme, Integer -> [Gpu.Memory] GpuFrame
  gr-render-node (gf) (w) (th) (depth) = when w.wn-kind
    is WkPanel -> gr-render-panel gf w th depth
    is WkLabel (txt) -> gr-render-label gf w th txt depth
    is WkButton (txt) -> gr-render-button gf w th txt depth
    is WkGauge (val) (max-val) -> gr-render-gauge gf w th val max-val depth
    is WkSeparator -> gr-render-separator gf w th depth
    is WkInput (txt) (cursor) -> gr-render-input gf w th txt cursor depth
    is WkCustom (tag) -> if tag == wk-scroll-view-tag then gf else gr-render-panel gf w th depth

  gr-render-panel : GpuFrame, WidgetNode, Theme, Integer -> [Gpu.Memory] GpuFrame
  gr-render-panel (gf) (w) (th) (depth) =
    let style = theme-resolve-panel th (w.wn-state)
    in let br = box-margin-rect (w.wn-bounds) (style.ws-margin)
    in gr-emit-box gf br (style.ws-bg) (style.ws-border) depth

  gr-render-button : GpuFrame, WidgetNode, Theme, Text, Integer -> [Gpu.Memory] GpuFrame
  gr-render-button (gf) (w) (th) (txt) (depth) =
    let style = theme-resolve-button th (w.wn-state)
    in let br = box-margin-rect (w.wn-bounds) (style.ws-margin)
    in let content = box-content-from-style (w.wn-bounds) style
    in let text-depth = if depth <= 2000 then gr-depth-top else depth - 1000
    in gr-emit-text (gr-emit-box gf br (style.ws-bg) (style.ws-border) depth) txt (content.lr-x) (content.lr-y) (theme-tone-fg th (w.wn-tone) (style.ws-fg)) text-depth (content.lr-w) text-overflow-default

  gr-render-label : GpuFrame, WidgetNode, Theme, Text, Integer -> [Gpu.Memory] GpuFrame
  gr-render-label (gf) (w) (th) (txt) (depth) =
    let style = theme-resolve-label th (w.wn-state)
    in let content = box-content-from-style (w.wn-bounds) style
    in let text-depth = if depth <= 2000 then gr-depth-top else depth - 1000
    in gr-emit-text gf txt (content.lr-x) (content.lr-y) (theme-tone-fg th (w.wn-tone) (style.ws-fg)) text-depth (content.lr-w) text-overflow-default

 The value is held inside its own bounds before the width is taken.
 Without that, a gauge handed a value above its maximum emits a fill
 wider than the box it was drawn in and paints over whatever sits
 beside it, and one handed a negative value emits a negative width.
 The framebuffer compositor has always clamped; this path had not, so
 the same widget tree drew differently depending on which renderer
 walked it.

  gr-render-gauge : GpuFrame, WidgetNode, Theme, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Gpu.Memory] GpuFrame
  gr-render-gauge (gf) (w) (th) (val) (max-val) (depth) =
    let style = theme-resolve-gauge th (w.wn-state)
    in let br = box-margin-rect (w.wn-bounds) (style.ws-margin)
    in let content = box-content-from-style (w.wn-bounds) style
    in let held = if val < 0 then 0 else if val > max-val then max-val else val
    in let fill-w = if max-val > 0 then content.lr-w * held / max-val else 0
    in gr-emit-rect (gr-emit-box gf br (style.ws-bg) (style.ws-border) depth) (content.lr-x) (content.lr-y) fill-w (content.lr-h) (style.ws-fg) (depth - 1)

  gr-render-separator : GpuFrame, WidgetNode, Theme, Integer -> [Gpu.Memory] GpuFrame
  gr-render-separator (gf) (w) (th) (depth) =
    let style = theme-resolve-separator th (w.wn-state)
    in let br = box-margin-rect (w.wn-bounds) (style.ws-margin)
    in gr-emit-rect gf (br.lr-x) (br.lr-y + br.lr-h / 2) (br.lr-w) 1 (style.ws-bg) depth

 The caret is held inside its own content box before it is drawn. Six
 pixels per character of cursor index leaves the widget as soon as the
 index passes the box width, and a caret drawn outside lands on whatever
 sits to the right. That is the same failure the gauge fill had one
 function above, and it is reachable the same way: `apps/cvmm` renders
 through `bare-app-render` and every filter box there sets its cursor to
 the length of the filter text, so the index grows with each keystroke.
 The low end is held too, because a negative index would paint to the
 left of the box. `codex/test/gpu-input-cursor` scans outside the widget,
 which is the only place the difference shows.

  gr-render-input : GpuFrame, WidgetNode, Theme, Text, Integer, Integer -> [Gpu.Memory] GpuFrame
  gr-render-input (gf) (w) (th) (txt) (cursor) (depth) =
    let style = theme-resolve-input th (w.wn-state)
    in let br = box-margin-rect (w.wn-bounds) (style.ws-margin)
    in let content = box-content-from-style (w.wn-bounds) style
    in let text-depth = if depth <= 2000 then gr-depth-top else depth - 1000
    in let cur-hi = content.lr-x + content.lr-w - 1
    in let raw-x = content.lr-x + cursor * 6
    in let capped = if raw-x > cur-hi then cur-hi else raw-x
    in let cursor-x = if capped < content.lr-x then content.lr-x else capped
    in gr-emit-rect (gr-emit-text (gr-emit-box gf br (style.ws-bg) (style.ws-border) depth) txt (content.lr-x) (content.lr-y) (style.ws-fg) text-depth (content.lr-w) text-overflow-default) cursor-x (content.lr-y) 1 7 (style.ws-fg) text-depth

Section: Overlay Rendering

  gr-render-overlay : GpuFrame, Overlay, Theme -> [Gpu.Memory] GpuFrame
  gr-render-overlay (gf) (ov) (th) =
    if ov.ov-visible then
      let laid = widget-layout (ov.ov-widget) th (layout-rect (ov.ov-x) (ov.ov-y) (ov.ov-width) (ov.ov-height))
      in gr-render-tree (gr-emit-rect gf (ov.ov-x) (ov.ov-y) (ov.ov-width) (ov.ov-height) ((th.th-palette).pal-bg) gr-depth-overlay) laid th gr-depth-overlay
    else gf

  gr-render-overlays : GpuFrame, OverlayStack, Theme, Integer, Integer -> [Gpu.Memory] GpuFrame
  gr-render-overlays (gf) (os) (th) (i) (n) =
    if i >= n then gf
    else gr-render-overlays (gr-render-overlay gf (list-at (os.os-overlays) i) th) os th (i + 1) n

Section: GPU Rect Emit

 gr-frame-bump takes the write count it does not need, because that is
 what lets the emit stay out of let-binding position: the effectful
 call becomes an argument to a pure function instead of a binding.

  gr-frame-bump : Integer, GpuFrame -> GpuFrame
  gr-frame-bump (written) (gf) = GpuFrame { gf-idx = gf.gf-idx + 2, gf-max = gf.gf-max }

  gr-emit-rect : GpuFrame, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Gpu.Memory] GpuFrame
  gr-emit-rect (gf) (x) (y) (w) (h) (color) (depth) =
    if w <= 0 then gf
    else if h <= 0 then gf
    else if gf.gf-idx + 2 > gf.gf-max then gf
    else gr-frame-bump (gr-rect (gf.gf-idx) x y w h color depth) gf

Section: Bordered Box

 A styled widget is a fill inside a border, and until 2026-07-21 this
 backend drew only the fill. Every built-in theme sets a one-pixel border
 and a panel background equal to the page background, so the border was the
 only thing distinguishing a panel from what it sat on -- and the CPU
 renderer in UI Render draws it (render-panel calls render-border, as do the
 gauge, button and input paths there). Two backends, one theme, and the one
 every bare-metal app uses silently discarded a style the other honoured.
 That is what left the GuiOS sidebar and taskbar without a visible extent
 after the depth defect was fixed: the geometry was in front of its parent,
 it was counted, and it was the colour of what it covered.

 The four edges and the fill are DISJOINT rects at ONE depth, rather than a
 full-bleed fill with a border laid over it. That is deliberate. The
 rasterizer's test is a strict depth < stored, so an overlapping border
 would have to take a depth step, and the step below a node is the one its
 children already occupy. Rects that do not overlap never contend, so no
 step is needed and the depth band is left alone.

 A zero-width side costs nothing -- gr-emit-rect answers its input unchanged
 for a non-positive extent -- so a border-none style emits exactly the one
 fill it always did.

 The five emits are one expression on one line for the reason given above
 gr-tri: each is effectful, an effectful value may not be let-bound
 (CDX2033), and an application broken across lines is CDX1070. The widths
 and the inner band are pure, so those are let-bound and the geometry reads
 off them.

  gr-emit-box : GpuFrame, LayoutRect, Integer, Border, Integer -> [Gpu.Memory] GpuFrame
  gr-emit-box (gf) (r) (bg) (bdr) (depth) =
    let wt = (bdr.bdr-top).brd-width
    in let wb = (bdr.bdr-bottom).brd-width
    in let wl = (bdr.bdr-left).brd-width
    in let wr = (bdr.bdr-right).brd-width
    in let iy = r.lr-y + wt
    in let ih = box-clamp0 (r.lr-h - wt - wb)
    in let iw = box-clamp0 (r.lr-w - wl - wr)
    in gr-emit-rect (gr-emit-rect (gr-emit-rect (gr-emit-rect (gr-emit-rect gf (r.lr-x + wl) iy iw ih bg depth) (r.lr-x) (r.lr-y) (r.lr-w) wt ((bdr.bdr-top).brd-color) depth) (r.lr-x) (r.lr-y + r.lr-h - wb) (r.lr-w) wb ((bdr.bdr-bottom).brd-color) depth) (r.lr-x) iy wl ih ((bdr.bdr-left).brd-color) depth) (r.lr-x + r.lr-w - wr) iy wr ih ((bdr.bdr-right).brd-color) depth

Section: 5x7 Bitmap Text

  gr-glyph-advance : Integer = 6

  gr-glyph-width : Integer = 5

  gr-emit-text : GpuFrame, Text, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, TextOverflow -> [Gpu.Memory] GpuFrame
  gr-emit-text (gf) (txt) (x) (y) (color) (depth) (avail) (mode) =
    let len = text-length txt
    in let start = text-overflow-start mode len avail gr-glyph-advance gr-glyph-width
    in let count = text-overflow-count mode len avail gr-glyph-advance gr-glyph-width
    in let dots = text-overflow-dots mode len avail gr-glyph-advance gr-glyph-width
    in gr-emit-dots (gr-emit-text-loop gf txt x y color depth start (start + count) start) (x + count * gr-glyph-advance) y color depth 0 dots

  gr-emit-text-loop : GpuFrame, Text, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Gpu.Memory] GpuFrame
  gr-emit-text-loop (gf) (txt) (x) (y) (color) (depth) (i) (stop) (start) =
    if i >= stop then gf
    else gr-emit-text-loop (gr-emit-glyph gf (gr-text-char-at txt i) (x + (i - start) * gr-glyph-advance) y color depth) txt x y color depth (i + 1) stop start

  gr-emit-dots : GpuFrame, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Gpu.Memory] GpuFrame
  gr-emit-dots (gf) (x) (y) (color) (depth) (k) (n) =
    if k >= n then gf
    else gr-emit-dots (gr-emit-glyph gf (gr-text-char-at "." 0) (x + k * gr-glyph-advance) y color depth) x y color depth (k + 1) n

  gr-emit-glyph : GpuFrame, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Gpu.Memory] GpuFrame
  gr-emit-glyph (gf) (ch) (x) (y) (color) (depth) =
    let rows = gr-glyph-rows ch
    in gr-emit-glyph-rows gf rows x y color depth 0 7

  gr-emit-glyph-rows : GpuFrame, List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Gpu.Memory] GpuFrame
  gr-emit-glyph-rows (gf) (rows) (x) (y) (color) (depth) (row) (max-row) =
    if row >= max-row then gf
    else gr-emit-glyph-rows (gr-emit-glyph-cols gf (list-at rows row) x (y + row) color depth 0 5) rows x y color depth (row + 1) max-row

  gr-emit-glyph-cols : GpuFrame, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Gpu.Memory] GpuFrame
  gr-emit-glyph-cols (gf) (bits) (x) (y) (color) (depth) (col) (max-col) =
    if col >= max-col then gf
    else if bit-and bits (bit-shl 1 (4 - col)) > 0 then
      gr-emit-glyph-cols (gr-emit-rect gf (x + col) y 1 1 color depth) bits x y color depth (col + 1) max-col
    else gr-emit-glyph-cols gf bits x y color depth (col + 1) max-col

Section: CCE to ASCII

  gr-text-char-at : Text, Integer -> Integer
  gr-text-char-at (t) (i) = gr-cce-to-ascii (char-code (char-at t i))

  gr-cce-to-ascii : Integer -> Integer
  gr-cce-to-ascii (c) =
    if c == 2 then 32
    else if c >= 3 then if c <= 12 then c + 45 else gr-cce-alpha c
    else 32

  gr-cce-alpha : Integer -> Integer
  gr-cce-alpha (c) =
    if c == 13 then 69 else if c == 14 then 84 else if c == 15 then 65
    else if c == 16 then 79 else if c == 17 then 73 else if c == 18 then 78
    else if c == 19 then 83 else if c == 20 then 72 else if c == 21 then 82
    else if c == 22 then 68 else if c == 23 then 76 else if c == 24 then 67
    else if c == 25 then 85 else if c == 26 then 77 else if c == 27 then 87
    else if c == 28 then 70 else if c == 29 then 71 else if c == 30 then 89
    else if c == 31 then 80 else if c == 32 then 66 else if c == 33 then 86
    else if c == 34 then 75 else if c == 35 then 74 else if c == 36 then 88
    else if c == 37 then 81 else if c == 38 then 90
    else if c >= 39 then if c <= 64 then gr-cce-alpha (c - 26) else gr-cce-punct c
    else 32

  gr-cce-punct : Integer -> Integer
  gr-cce-punct (c) =
    if c == 65 then 46 else if c == 66 then 44 else if c == 67 then 33
    else if c == 68 then 63 else if c == 69 then 58 else if c == 70 then 59
    else if c == 71 then 39 else if c == 73 then 45 else if c == 74 then 40
    else if c == 75 then 41 else if c == 76 then 43 else if c == 77 then 61
    else if c == 78 then 42 else if c == 81 then 47
    else 32

Section: Glyph Data

  gr-glyph-rows : Integer -> List Integer
  gr-glyph-rows (ch) =
    if ch == 48 then [14, 17, 19, 21, 25, 17, 14]
    else if ch == 49 then [4, 12, 4, 4, 4, 4, 14]
    else if ch == 50 then [14, 17, 1, 2, 4, 8, 31]
    else if ch == 51 then [14, 17, 1, 6, 1, 17, 14]
    else if ch == 52 then [2, 6, 10, 18, 31, 2, 2]
    else if ch == 53 then [31, 16, 30, 1, 1, 17, 14]
    else if ch == 54 then [6, 8, 16, 30, 17, 17, 14]
    else if ch == 55 then [31, 1, 2, 4, 4, 4, 4]
    else if ch == 56 then [14, 17, 17, 14, 17, 17, 14]
    else if ch == 57 then [14, 17, 17, 15, 1, 2, 12]
    else gr-glyph-alpha ch

  gr-glyph-alpha : Integer -> List Integer
  gr-glyph-alpha (ch) =
    if ch == 65 then [14, 17, 17, 31, 17, 17, 17]
    else if ch == 66 then [30, 17, 17, 30, 17, 17, 30]
    else if ch == 67 then [14, 17, 16, 16, 16, 17, 14]
    else if ch == 68 then [30, 17, 17, 17, 17, 17, 30]
    else if ch == 69 then [31, 16, 16, 30, 16, 16, 31]
    else if ch == 70 then [31, 16, 16, 30, 16, 16, 16]
    else if ch == 71 then [14, 17, 16, 19, 17, 17, 14]
    else if ch == 72 then [17, 17, 17, 31, 17, 17, 17]
    else if ch == 73 then [14, 4, 4, 4, 4, 4, 14]
    else if ch == 74 then [7, 2, 2, 2, 2, 18, 12]
    else if ch == 75 then [17, 18, 20, 24, 20, 18, 17]
    else if ch == 76 then [16, 16, 16, 16, 16, 16, 31]
    else if ch == 77 then [17, 27, 21, 21, 17, 17, 17]
    else gr-glyph-alpha2 ch

  gr-glyph-alpha2 : Integer -> List Integer
  gr-glyph-alpha2 (ch) =
    if ch == 78 then [17, 25, 21, 19, 17, 17, 17]
    else if ch == 79 then [14, 17, 17, 17, 17, 17, 14]
    else if ch == 80 then [30, 17, 17, 30, 16, 16, 16]
    else if ch == 81 then [14, 17, 17, 17, 21, 18, 13]
    else if ch == 82 then [30, 17, 17, 30, 20, 18, 17]
    else if ch == 83 then [14, 17, 16, 14, 1, 17, 14]
    else if ch == 84 then [31, 4, 4, 4, 4, 4, 4]
    else if ch == 85 then [17, 17, 17, 17, 17, 17, 14]
    else if ch == 86 then [17, 17, 17, 17, 10, 10, 4]
    else if ch == 87 then [17, 17, 17, 21, 21, 27, 17]
    else if ch == 88 then [17, 17, 10, 4, 10, 17, 17]
    else if ch == 89 then [17, 17, 10, 4, 4, 4, 4]
    else if ch == 90 then [31, 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 31]
    else gr-glyph-misc ch

  gr-glyph-misc : Integer -> List Integer
  gr-glyph-misc (ch) =
    if ch == 46 then [0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 4]
    else if ch == 44 then [0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 4, 8]
    else if ch == 58 then [0, 0, 4, 0, 0, 4, 0]
    else if ch == 45 then [0, 0, 0, 14, 0, 0, 0]
    else if ch == 43 then [0, 4, 4, 31, 4, 4, 0]
    else if ch == 42 then [0, 4, 21, 14, 21, 4, 0]
    else if ch == 47 then [1, 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 16]
    else if ch == 40 then [2, 4, 8, 8, 8, 4, 2]
    else if ch == 41 then [8, 4, 2, 2, 2, 4, 8]
    else if ch == 91 then [14, 8, 8, 8, 8, 8, 14]
    else if ch == 93 then [14, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 14]
    else if ch == 61 then [0, 0, 31, 0, 31, 0, 0]
    else if ch == 60 then [2, 4, 8, 16, 8, 4, 2]
    else if ch == 62 then [8, 4, 2, 1, 2, 4, 8]
    else if ch == 32 then [0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0]
    else if ch == 95 then [0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 31]
    else if ch == 35 then [10, 10, 31, 10, 31, 10, 10]
    else if ch == 33 then [4, 4, 4, 4, 0, 0, 4]
    else if ch == 63 then [14, 17, 1, 2, 4, 0, 4]
    else if ch == 37 then [24, 25, 2, 4, 8, 19, 3]
    else gr-glyph-lower ch

  gr-glyph-lower : Integer -> List Integer
  gr-glyph-lower (ch) =
    if ch == 97 then [0, 0, 14, 1, 15, 17, 15]
    else if ch == 98 then [16, 16, 30, 17, 17, 17, 30]
    else if ch == 99 then [0, 0, 14, 16, 16, 17, 14]
    else if ch == 100 then [1, 1, 15, 17, 17, 17, 15]
    else if ch == 101 then [0, 0, 14, 17, 31, 16, 14]
    else if ch == 102 then [6, 8, 28, 8, 8, 8, 8]
    else if ch == 103 then [0, 0, 15, 17, 15, 1, 14]
    else if ch == 104 then [16, 16, 30, 17, 17, 17, 17]
    else if ch == 105 then [4, 0, 12, 4, 4, 4, 14]
